+llama: the encoder output of an encoder-decoder model is kept per sequence (llama_memory_enc_dec)
+
+Stage 1 of docs/notes/llama-cpp-encoder-decoder.md (Louise), on top of
+0003 (the server runs the encoder) and 0004 (slots encoded in waves). The
+library kept ONE encoder output per context (llama_context::cross) and every
+llama_encode replaced it whole, so a slot could not be encoded while others
+decoded. T5 now gets its own memory: the decoder's llama_kv_cache plus a
+host-side store of encoder rows owned by the sequences they were encoded
+for. An encode replaces the rows of the batch's sequences and keeps every
+other sequence's; a decoder ubatch uploads the rows its sequences own and
+masks each token to its own sequence's rows; a sequence with no rows is
+refused (a new internal status, LLAMA_MEMORY_STATUS_FAILED_INPUT, -1 from
+llama_decode). A whole-sequence removal, clear, and seq_keep release the
+rows; seq_cp shares them; the sequence state carries them as a suffix
+(magic T5XE). For n_seq_max > 1 the context forces kv_unified, so the mask
+and the KV views agree. No public API changes. Only T5 constructs the
+memory; llama_cross and the graph parameter stay for the carried patch.
+
+Server (0004's waves, unchanged in shape): a wave is encoded while earlier
+ones decode (a late request joins the next step instead of waiting), the
+prompt start keeps the rows the wave just stored, the load-time probe
+encode and the partial-removal probe (which decodes after a clear) are
+gone for these models, and the forced unified cache is mirrored in the
+server's bookkeeping.
+
+Checked on the CPU (random-weight T5, tests/test-llama-archs -a '^t5$',
+tests/test-enc-dec-store): a sequence alone gives the bytes of a
+one-sequence context; two sequences decoding in one batch are each within
+nmse 1e-4 of their solo runs (not bitwise, as predicted); a whole removal,
+a clear and a truncated state restore leave -1 without an abort; a partial
+removal, seq_cp and a restored state keep the output. The tiny-model
+server checks of 0004 pass (lone request byte-identical to 0003, the same
+7 prompts in 3 orders, 60 requests after a burst); the late request is
+answered in 0.06 s instead of after the wave.
+
